// ---------- 1 Introduction ----------
const intro = [
  H1("1. Introduction"),
  P("Data centres used about 1–1.5% of global electricity around 2020 [[cite: masanet_recalibrating_2020]], and their demand is now growing quickly with artificial intelligence [[cite: noauthor_energy_2025]]. Regulators have begun to require operators to report energy use and its sources [[cite: european_commission_commission_2024]]. Carbon-aware computing reduces emissions without new hardware: because the carbon intensity of grid electricity varies by hour and by place, flexible work can run when or where electricity is cleaner [[cite: wiesner_lets_2021]] [[cite: radovanovic_carbon-aware_2023]]. Scientific workflows are natural candidates. They are large graphs of batch tasks (DAGs), and users often accept a deadline later than the shortest possible completion time. The slack between the two can be used to move tasks into cleaner periods."),
  P("A recent line of work schedules workflows with exactly this aim. CaWoSched shifts the tasks of a fixed mapping to reduce the energy drawn above a time-varying green power budget [[cite: schweisgut_carbon-aware_2025]]. CWM maps and schedules jointly on heterogeneous clusters and reports a median reduction of its carbon cost of 69% relative to HEFT when the deadline is twice the makespan [[cite: schweisgut_carbon-aware_2026]]. Precedence-aware schedulers for data-processing clusters defer non-critical tasks during high-carbon periods [[cite: lechowicz_carbon-_2025]], and trace-driven studies estimate savings above 80% for workflows that can wait for days [[cite: west_systematic_2026]]. These evaluations share one assumption: the scheduler knows the future carbon intensity, or a budget derived from it, exactly."),
  P("In practice a scheduler can know only a forecast. Grid operators and research groups publish forecasts from a few hours to four days ahead [[cite: bruce_carbon_2021]] [[cite: maji_multi-day_2023]], and their errors grow with the horizon. Studies of forecast error in carbon-aware computing either add synthetic noise to the actual signal [[cite: wiesner_lets_2021]] [[cite: sukprasert_limitations_2024]], or use forecasts that a model produced after the fact [[cite: li_uncertainty-aware_2024]] [[cite: joseph_litecast_2025]]. They also consider independent jobs. Workflows differ. A workflow plan contains hundreds of deferral decisions made on the same forecast, a deferred task delays its successors, and all tasks share one deadline. A forecast error can therefore turn a plan that looks greener than the carbon-agnostic one into a plan that emits more. We call such an outcome a **carbon trap**. Deferral also uses up the slack that would otherwise absorb tasks that run longer than planned."),
  P("This paper asks four questions. How much of the oracle saving of carbon-aware workflow schedulers survives the forecasts that were actually issued? How often do their plans become carbon traps, and can traps be reduced cheaply? Does re-planning on newer forecasts remove the problem? And what happens to deadlines when task runtimes vary? We answer them in a sequence of pre-registered studies. The main study replays 3,000 WfCommons workflow jobs [[cite: coleman_wfcommons_2022]] against the 48-hour forecast vintages published by the Great Britain grid operator, archived as they were issued. It compares oracle intensity, synthetic noise and issued forecasts for our list and local-search schedulers, whole-workflow shifting and the official code of CaWoSched. Three further studies extend the evaluation. The first covers four grids in the United States and Germany with model forecasts and adds the official code of CWM (4,800 jobs). The second uses a heterogeneous platform with idle power and CWM's own processor types (1,600 jobs). The third, a robustness study, tests re-planning, a work-conserving executor, slack reservation and alternative gates (1,200 jobs). Fig. 1 gives an overview of the evaluation framework and of the components we propose."),
  ...figure("fig0_framework.png", 600, 285,
    "**Fig. 1.** Overview of the evaluation and of the proposed components. (a) A workflow (DAG) with a deadline D. (b) The realised carbon intensity and forecast vintages issued at different times. (c) A carbon-aware plan (filled) is built on the forecast and compared with HEFT (dashed); the proposed gate allows deferrals whose forecast gain exceeds the calibrated error (green) and blocks the others (red), and the proposed slack reserve plans against D′ < D. (d) The plan is executed against the realised intensity with runtime overruns, which the reserve absorbs. (e) Distribution of per-run savings of CA-LS with issued GB forecasts (f ≥ 1.5, main study); runs below zero emit more than HEFT, and those more than 1% above it are carbon traps (red)."),
  P("«INTRO_RESULTS»"),
  P("The paper makes four contributions."),
  bullet("To our knowledge, the first evaluation of carbon-aware DAG schedulers, including the official CaWoSched and CWM codes, against forecast vintages that were actually issued, rather than oracle or synthetic signals."),
  bullet("Evidence from five grids that forecast error turns a sizeable share of workflow plans into carbon traps, that re-planning on newer forecasts does not remove them, and that across grids the loss is associated with the forecast error relative to the short-term variability of carbon intensity."),
  bullet("A lightweight gate calibrated on forecast-difference errors, with a per-decision guarantee (Proposition 1) and empirical coverage close to nominal, that reduces traps on every grid; evidence that more elaborate planners (re-planning, planning on an upper quantile, scenario-based risk control) do not reduce traps further; and evidence that a simple slack reserve removes most of the deadline risk of carbon-aware plans."),
  bullet("A transparent protocol with sealed analysis plans, a decision timeline, logged deviations and open code."),
  P("Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 defines the model and outcomes, Section 4 the schedulers and the gate, and Section 5 the data and study design. Section 6 reports the results, Section 7 discusses their implications and limitations, and Section 8 concludes."),
];

// ---------- 2 Related work ----------
const related = [
  H1("2. Background and related work"),
  H2("2.1 Shifting independent workloads, with and without forecast uncertainty"),
  P("Early work on geographical load balancing routed requests to regions with cleaner or cheaper power [[cite: liu_greening_2011]] [[cite: gao_its_2012]] [[cite: zhou_carbon-aware_2016]], and green datacentres matched deferrable load to on-site renewable supply [[cite: goiri_matching_2015]]. Wiesner et al. [[cite: wiesner_lets_2021]] made temporal shifting concrete for the cloud, with trace-driven savings of up to about a third for flexible nightly jobs; Gaussian forecast noise of 5–10% cost them one to two percentage points. Sukprasert et al. [[cite: sukprasert_limitations_2024]] showed with 123 regions that realistic slack yields much smaller savings than idealised settings, and that a forecast error of about 14% MAPE raises emissions by around 3%. A companion study found that optimising for average or for marginal intensity can lead to opposite decisions [[cite: sukprasert_implications_2024]]. Google's production system sets hourly capacity limits for flexible batch work from day-ahead forecasts, inflating demand by a high quantile of recent forecast errors [[cite: radovanovic_carbon-aware_2023]]. Related systems scale resources with carbon intensity [[cite: hanafy_carbonscaler_2023]], manage real-time workloads on renewable-driven cores [[cite: hewage_framework_2025]], migrate tasks across datacentres in space and time [[cite: yang_carbon_2023]], trade carbon against cost [[cite: hanafy_going_2024]] or exploit spatial variation at the edge [[cite: wu_carbonedge_2025]] [[cite: son_greenscale_2025]] [[cite: perin_towards_2022]]; surveys organise the field [[cite: tirel_workload_2026]] [[cite: gill_taxonomy_2019]], and system-level studies caution that flexible datacentres do not always lower emissions [[cite: senga_flexible_2026]]."),
  P("A second line of work designs algorithms that tolerate uncertain signals, again for a single deferrable job. Online pause-and-resume algorithms decide from the current signal and its known bounds [[cite: lechowicz_online_2023]]. Learning-augmented algorithms add predictions while keeping worst-case guarantees [[cite: bostandoost_lacs_2024]] [[cite: lechowicz_learning-augmented_2025]] [[cite: johnson_signal-aware_2025]], and algorithm selection picks the best heuristic per region [[cite: bostandoost_data-driven_2024]]. Closest to our work, Li et al. [[cite: li_uncertainty-aware_2024]] wrap CarbonCast forecasts in sequential conformal intervals. They report that point forecasts pick the wrong hours on 10.6–16.8% of days, raising emissions by 2.7–7.3%, and that acting on the intervals avoids most of this increase. Learned schedulers that integrate carbon forecasts into workload scheduling have also been proposed [[cite: sohrabbeig_learning_2026]]. These studies establish that forecast error matters, but for independent jobs, and with forecasts produced by a model after the fact."),
  H2("2.2 Carbon-aware scheduling of DAGs and workflows"),
  P("Workflow scheduling has a long history in deadline- and cost-constrained cloud settings [[cite: abrishami_deadline-constrained_2013]] [[cite: rodriguez_deadline_2014]] [[cite: rodriguez_taxonomy_2017]], with HEFT as the standard list-scheduling reference [[cite: topcuoglu_performance-effective_2002]]. Environment-conscious scheduling of HPC applications [[cite: garg_environment-conscious_2011]] and renewable-aware multi-cloud placement [[cite: xu_managing_2020]] introduced carbon as an objective. CaWoSched [[cite: schweisgut_carbon-aware_2025]] fixes the mapping and processor order and shifts start times to minimise the energy drawn above a green power budget under a hard deadline. CWM [[cite: schweisgut_carbon-aware_2026]] maps and schedules jointly on heterogeneous clusters with idle power. PCAPS [[cite: lechowicz_carbon-_2025]] defers non-critical Spark tasks during high-carbon periods, and Bostandoost et al. [[cite: bostandoost_quantifying_2026]] compute exact bounds for small DAG instances as a job-shop problem. West et al. [[cite: west_systematic_2026]] quantify shifting, interruption and scaling for seven Nextflow workflows and find large savings when workflows can wait for days; their sensitivity analysis with 5–15% synthetic forecast error shows small shortfalls. With that exception, these studies assume the future signal is known, and only West et al. vary task runtimes, in a post hoc check."),
  H2("2.3 Carbon-intensity forecasts"),
  P("The GB operator issues a 48-h national forecast every 30 minutes [[cite: bruce_carbon_2021]]. Research forecasters extend the horizon: DACF predicts one day ahead [[cite: maji_dacf_2022]], CarbonCast up to 96 h with regional MAPEs of a few percent to about 20% [[cite: maji_multi-day_2023]], EnsembleCI improves on CarbonCast by about a fifth [[cite: yan_ensembleci_2025]], and LiteCast trains on one week of history [[cite: joseph_litecast_2025]]. LiteCast's authors show that a forecast's ranking of hours, rather than its point accuracy, determines the saving of a single job. Forecasters are usually judged by accuracy metrics, not by the decisions they induce, and scheduling studies usually regenerate forecasts with a model rather than replaying what an operator published."),
  H2("2.4 Robust DAG scheduling and conformal calibration"),
  P("Robust DAG scheduling studies plans under uncertain durations; slack absorbs overruns, and makespan variability trades off against it [[cite: canon_evaluation_2010]] [[cite: shi_robust_2006]] [[cite: lombardi_robust_2013]] [[cite: taghinezhad-niar_qos-aware_2022]]. Carbon-aware schedulers spend exactly this slack, but they have not been evaluated under runtime variation. Conformal prediction gives distribution-free error bounds from a calibration set [[cite: angelopoulos_conformal_2024]], extended to time series [[cite: xu_conformal_2021]] and to distribution shift [[cite: gibbs_adaptive_2021]]. Our gate uses an empirical quantile of recent errors in this spirit, but of forecast differences rather than levels, because a deferral compares two windows."),
  H2("2.5 Positioning"),
  P("Table 1 compares the closest studies with ours. No prior study combines deadline-constrained DAG scheduling, forecasts that were actually issued, runtime variability, the official code of the closest published schedulers and a pre-registered evaluation."),
  caption("**Table 1.** Closest related work and this study. \"Synthetic\" denotes noise added to actual intensity; \"model forecasts\" are produced by a forecasting model after the fact, as opposed to vintages issued at the time."),
  t1,
];

