// ---------- 7 Discussion ----------
const discussion = [
  H1("7. Discussion"),
  H2("7.1 Why forecasts hurt workflow deferral"),
  P("Each deferral pays off only if the forecast ranks the two windows correctly, so the error that matters is the error of a difference between windows, not of a level. A workflow plan makes many deferrals on one vintage; their errors are correlated rather than averaged out, and the safeguard, which relies on the same vintage, cannot detect them. Proposition 1 bounds the risk of each deferral but not of the plan, which is why a gate reduces traps without removing them. Three further observations point to the forecast at submission as the bottleneck. Re-planning on newer vintages improved the optimisation but added little information (Section 6.3); planning on a pessimistic forecast changed little, because a horizon-dependent bias shifts nearby windows together; and a planner that controls the trap risk of the whole plan over error scenarios did no better than the gate (Section 6.4). Across grids, how much of the saving survived was associated with the forecast error relative to the short-term variability of intensity more than with the relative error (exploratory, Fig. 8a). This agrees with the observation by Joseph et al. [[cite: joseph_litecast_2025]] that ranking quality matters more than point accuracy, and it suggests that accuracy metrics such as MAPE [[cite: maji_multi-day_2023]] [[cite: yan_ensembleci_2025]] are a poor guide to the value of a forecast for scheduling."),
  H2("7.2 Deadlines"),
  P("A carbon-aware plan spends the slack that, in a carbon-agnostic plan, absorbs overruns [[cite: canon_evaluation_2010]] [[cite: shi_robust_2006]]. With moderate runtime noise this raised deadline misses by 28–49 percentage points on the homogeneous platform (GB and the four-grid extension), and a work-conserving executor reduced but did not remove the gap (Section 6.5). For moderate noise the remedy is cheap. «DISC_RESERVE» Evaluations of carbon-aware schedulers should report deadline misses under runtime variation alongside savings."),
  H2("7.3 Recommendations"),
  P("We separate recommendations backed by sealed tests [C] from those based on exploratory or robustness analyses [E]. (1) Evaluate carbon-aware schedulers on forecasts that were actually issued, not on hindsight [C]; i.i.d. synthetic noise misstated the trap risk [E]. (2) Gate deferrals by a calibrated bound on forecast-difference errors; it reduced traps in every sealed test [C] and on every grid [E], and simpler gates did not dominate it [E]. (3) Reserve part of the slack, sized to the runtime variability of the workflow [E]. (4) Before deploying, measure the forecast error relative to the short-term variability of intensity (κ); where κ approaches one, expected gains are small and traps frequent [E]. (5) Compare against whole-workflow shifting: on homogeneous platforms it matched the forecast-based saving of task-level scheduling, with fewer deadline misses, and is far simpler [E]; on heterogeneous platforms, energy-aware mapping matters more than carbon timing [E]. (6) Report the split of savings into an energy part and a timing part, since only the latter depends on forecasts [E]."),
  H2("7.4 Threats to validity"),
  P("**Internal.** All schedulers run in one simulator at one-minute resolution without data-transfer time. On the homogeneous platform idle power is not attributed, which assumes idle machines are powered down or serve other work. If they serve other work, deferral may displace it, an effect we do not model [[cite: senga_flexible_2026]]. The official codes required adapters (Appendix A). Local search uses a fixed budget of 2|V| moves; repeated optimisation raised the oracle saving by 1.4 points (Section 6.3), so absolute savings are conservative, although the shares kept under forecasts changed little. Our budget mapping was chosen because it gave the tools their best oracle saving, and the authors' narrower band was also tested. **External.** Issued vintages were available for one grid and one season. The other grids use model forecasts from 2021, which may be better or worse than operator forecasts. The development year suggests the loss is present in every season, but it uses an earlier configuration without the safeguard. The instances are WfCommons workflows, and most are limited by capacity rather than by their critical path; per-workflow results did not depend on structure. On clusters of 32 and 64 machines (exploratory, seed 10, Supplementary Table S6), CA-LS kept a similar share of its oracle saving (R = 0.79 against 0.80 on 8 machines), traps became more frequent (7.4% and 9.7% against 5.5%) and the gate still cut them to 2.2–2.5%; the 25% reserve was less effective there (16–28% misses), because HEFT itself started to miss deadlines at f = 1.2. Communication-heavy workflows may behave differently. We use the average intensity published by the operators, and the realised values are themselves estimates; marginal signals can lead to different decisions [[cite: sukprasert_implications_2024]] [[cite: wiesner_moving_2025]], as can location- versus market-based accounting [[cite: maji_green_2024]], and only operational emissions are counted [[cite: bashir_sunk_2024]]. **Construct.** Traps use a 1% margin and R is a ratio of means; we therefore report other margins, the expected excess emission, R per f, absolute losses and native objectives. **Statistical.** The sealed analyses cluster by submission time. Clustering by week and correcting for multiple tests weakens one verdict (H1 on GB), which the four-grid extension confirms robustly. Pooled results of the extension average over very different grids, and the grid-month analysis rests on few submissions per month. The synthetic-noise regime is i.i.d., and runtime noise is i.i.d. per task except in one exploratory check. Two of five hypotheses on the heterogeneous platform and one of five in the main study were not supported, and several analyses are exploratory; we label them as such."),
];

